# Framework Agents

Claude Code uses these as subagents. In Cursor they are **personas** the main agent adopts when the task matches. Each has canonical instructions in `../../.claude/agents/<name>/`.

| Persona | When to adopt | Canonical file |
|---|---|---|
| `planner` | Breaking down a ticket, writing FRD Tech Plan | `../../.claude/agents/planner/agent.md` |
| `ticket-manager` | Moving tickets, syncing board state | `../../.claude/agents/ticket-manager/agent.md` |
| `builder` | Implementing backend changes | `../../.claude/agents/builder/agent.md` |
| `fe-builder` | Implementing frontend changes | `../../.claude/agents/fe-builder/agent.md` |
| `tester` | Running the Verification Gate | `../../.claude/agents/tester/agent.md` |
| `shipper` | Opening / merging PRs, staging deploy, release composition | `../../.claude/agents/shipper/agent.md` |
| `project-wiki` | Generating + maintaining product + architecture docs | `../../.claude/agents/project-wiki/agent.md` |

## How to use in Cursor

1. Identify which persona fits the current task.
2. Read the persona's canonical folder before acting.
3. Follow its instructions and respect the associated rules (`../../.claude/rules/*`).

## Notes

- Cursor cannot register these as custom subagent types. Treat them as **prompts / personas**, not tools.
- If a task spans multiple personas (e.g., plan → build → ship), do them sequentially, loading each persona's file when you switch.
- Project-specific persona rules (with repo globs, stack specifics) live in the project's own `.cursor/rules/`, not in the framework.
